@TsengSR Yes it would and maybe should for the reasons you note. Depending on how much you have time (or maybe I should?) the quickest option is to change the hardcoded path and then have the change announced in release notes and tell about it to people when something breaks and they come to ask.

The "right way" would be to refactor support to https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.data.common.dbproviderfactories?view=netframework-4.8 now that it is back in .NET Core and Orleans is on newer .NET Core also. This is how it worked before.

Going a bit further, and/or alternatively, expand the configuration surface so that one can supply a lambda function and one case being to load the connector library as needed and can set a few attributes (some set by default as currently). The default attributes is the ones like asynchronity you note, look at https://github.com/dotnet/orleans/blob/master/src/AdoNet/Shared/Storage/RelationalStorage.cs#L274 (the other one is streaming). It appears that besides this particular MySql issue, connector libraries may have version specific bugs also.

@TsengSR Do you have time and inclination to at minimum change the hardcoded library or refator even more?

@sergeybykov If just switching the hardcoded library, would it be OK to have this "small breakage"?

I would be willing to address this, but I'm preparing to some business events and travelling and can get back to this sometime on the first week of February.
